     5	## Why
     6	
     7	Four Sprint-11 surfaces need persistent state scoped to a specific plan:
     8	
     9	- Phase 7: shared scratchpad (agent → agent within the same plan)
    10	- Phase 4: self-improving skill proposals (pending SP-NNN records)
    11	- Phase 2: skill-retrieval index metadata (mtime, checksum)
    12	- Phase 11: session graph snapshots
    13	
    14	Each one would otherwise invent its own file layout, filelock strategy,
    15	redaction path, and audit-log event. Consolidating the mechanics here
    16	keeps invariants consistent across surfaces:
    17	
    18	1. Plan-scoped — writes/reads in one plan can't bleed into another.
    19	2. Redacted — every value goes through `_lib.redact.redact_secrets` on
    20	   the way in; secrets never reach the sqlite file.
    21	3. TTL + prune — stale values age out without manual intervention.
    22	4. Audit-logged — every write / read / prune emits a typed event so
    23	   `audit-query.py` can surface what each plan is doing.
    24	5. Filelock-safe — concurrent writers across processes get kernel-level
    25	   mutual exclusion via `_lib.filelock.FileLock`.
    26	
    27	## Path convention
    28	
    29	```
    30	${CEO_STATE_ROOT:-$HOME/.claude/projects/<native-slug>/state}/
    31	    <store_name>/
    32	        <plan_id>.sqlite        # the key/value db
    33	        <plan_id>.sqlite.lock   # the filelock sibling
    34	```
    35	
    36	`store_name` is a short slug chosen by the caller (`scratchpad`,
    37	`skill_proposals`, `skill_index`, `session_graph`). `plan_id` is the
    38	canonical `PLAN-NNN` string.
    39	
